# Legacy CMS URL repair. These sites are static exports of the old Omeka
# installs, and the export appended .html to every page: /items/show/60 became
# /items/show/60.html. Everything indexed or cited before the migration points
# at the extensionless form. Proven on occupyarchive.org: 272 redirects, 190
# hits.
#
# Ordering matters — Caddy runs `redir` before `handle`, so these fire ahead of
# the object-storage matcher, and among the `redir`s below file order decides.
# The specific rules must therefore stay above the generic one at the bottom.
#
# This site takes over virginiaslostat.org itself, so these rules also decide
# whether every citation printed before the migration still resolves. Each was
# exercised against a running Caddy 2.10.2 serving this directory.
@needs_html {
	not path *.*       # already has an extension: .html (no loop), assets
	not path */        # directories already resolve to index.html
	not path /files/*  # Garage-served; extensionless hashes must reach their handler
}

# On /files/*: the crawl excludes files/original|fullsize|thumbnails|
# square_thumbnails (see .gitignore), so those reach Garage through the
# not-found handler — which only runs once every `redir` has declined. This
# site's own file URLs all carry extensions, so `not path *.*` happens to cover
# them, but pandemicreligion.org, collectingthesetimes.org and
# www.preachinggoesviral.org serve /files/original/<hash> with no extension at
# all. That URL style and this rule cannot coexist: the 301 to <hash>.html wins
# and the object never gets served. Exclude the prefix outright rather than
# leave a landmine for whoever adds redirects to this site next.

# The old Omeka search endpoint. Nothing on the site ever linked to it — the
# theme rendered no search box at all — but it answered 200 for years, so
# bookmarks and referrer logs exist. search.html reads the same `query=`
# parameter Omeka used, so the search survives the hop.
@omeka_search path /search /search/
redir @omeka_search /search.html?{query} 302

# Tag browsing. The export captured 52 of these as real files whose NAME holds
# the query (`items/browse?tags=Fries.html`), which cannot be addressed through
# a redirect without re-encoding the '?'. items/search.html accepts `tags=` —
# the field name the old advanced-search form used — so the tag arrives as a
# live facet instead, which also covers the tags whose listing ran to several
# pages. Must stay above the generic rule, which drops the query.
@omeka_tag_browse {
	path /items/browse /items/browse/ /items/browse/page/*
	query tags=*
}
redir @omeka_tag_browse /items/search.html?tags={http.request.uri.query.tags} 302

# Two shapes the generic rule cannot serve.
#
# /items and /items/ — Omeka's own all-items URL. The export has no items.html,
# so the generic rule would 301 it into a 404; items/browse.html is the page it
# means.
#
# The trailing-slash forms are spelled out because `not path */` makes
# @needs_html skip them entirely — they fall through to the file server, find no
# index.html in items/ or items/browse/, and 404 on their own. Exact paths, not
# /items/*: items/show/<id>.html and items/browse/page/<n>.html are real files
# and a wildcard here would shadow every one of them.
@browse_landing path /items /items/ /items/browse/
redir @browse_landing /items/browse.html 301

# {path} drops the query, so every /items/browse?advanced[0][…] facet collapses
# onto the bare path — the right call, since those facets mean nothing against a
# frozen export, and the one that does still mean something (tags=) is handled
# above.
#
# NOT for Hugo sites — those need /foo/, not /foo.html, and this would 301 them
# into a 404.
redir @needs_html {path}.html 301
